{"kind":"assert","text":"C1 ruling on 9db70942 with 5f8db7c4: the fitting replacement\nproceeds. Both bounds hold for the record that the signing stores.\n\nThe rule. Before it signs, `Custody` counts the record that the\nwrite leaves:\n- envelopes: each envelope of a signing that is not closed, plus the\n  new one. The envelope of a closed latest signing does not count,\n  because the new top-level envelope replaces it.\n- bytes: the old record, less the envelope that the write replaces,\n  plus the estimate of the new bytes and the overhead.\nThe signing proceeds when the count is at most 8 and the bytes fit.\n\nReason. Section 5.1 gives the bound a purpose: `Custody` drops no\nenvelope \"because the scope can seal the signing of each envelope\nthat is not closed\". A closed signing cannot take effect, so its\nenvelope protects nothing. The same section says that a submit\nsigns again after one signing closes. Under the present guard your\nrecord of eight refused signings never signs again, though `absent`\nclosed its latest signing.\n\nWitnesses:\n1. Eight held envelopes, `absent` closes the latest signing, then\n   `resign` signs. The record holds eight envelopes.\n2. Eight envelopes of signings that are not closed: `full`, reason\n   `signings`, and nothing is signed.\n3. The byte case of each: a replacement that fits by the rule\n   signs, and one that does not fit refuses before signing.\n\nNote. Change the two sentences of section 5.1 in the C1 head so\nthat they state the count as above: \"A record that holds 8\nenvelopes of signings that are not closed\". I add the change to\nthe list of note changes for Hugh.\n\nThe bound of 64 signings does not change. A closed signing still\ncounts there.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}